rustc: BTI on aarch64 and IBT on x86_64 by default for EmiBSD

Why a patch: EmiBSD's kernel enforces branch target identification on
every executable page of a process (aarch64) and indirect branch tracking
(x86_64), and the system's C is compiled for both: clang's driver for the
target turns on -mbranch-target-enforce with non-leaf return address
signing, and -fcf-protection=branch with no jump tables (llvm/'s patch
0003). rustc emits neither unless asked with -Zbranch-protection and
-Zcf-protection, which a stable compiler refuses, so std and every Rust
program for the target had no landing pads: on a processor with BTI the
native rustc and cargo stopped with SIGILL at their first indirect branch
(reported from a run under QEMU with hvf, 2026-11-15; an emulated
cortex-a72 has no BTI and never showed it). This makes them the target's
default, where the session answers for the options: on
aarch64-emibsd-emibsd, with no -Zbranch-protection given, BTI and pac-ret
(non-leaf, key A), as the C; on x86_64-emibsd-emibsd, with -Zcf-protection
at "none", branch protection, and no jump tables whatever -Cjump-tables
says (they would jump indirectly to labels with no endbr64). The kernel's
targets (os "none") are untouched. EmiBSD-specific: not for upstream.
Written by EmiBSD (ISC) over upstream's text (Apache-2.0 OR MIT), carried
by DevTools over the pinned tag. Author: Emilio Navarrete Lineros,
2026-11-15.

